  README file for the CHICKEN Scheme system
  =========================================


  (c) 2008, The CHICKEN Team
  (c) 2000-2007, Felix L. Winkelmann

  version 6.0.1pre1


 1. Introduction

    CHICKEN is a Scheme-to-C compiler supporting the language
    features as defined in the 'Revised^5 Report on
    Scheme'. Separate compilation is supported and full
    tail-recursion and efficient first-class continuations are
    available.

    Some things that CHICKEN has to offer:

    1. CHICKEN generates quite portable C code and compiled files
       generated by it (including itself) should work without any
       changes on Windows, most UNIX-like platforms, and with
       minor changes on other systems.

    2. The whole package is distributed under a BSD style license
       and as such is free to use and modify as long as you agree
       to its terms.

    3. Linkage to C modules and C library functions is
       straightforward. Compiled programs can easily be embedded
       into existing C code.

    4. Loads of extra libraries.

    Note: Should you have any trouble in setting up and using
    CHICKEN, please ask questions on the CHICKEN users mailing
    list.  Check http://wiki.call-cc.org/discussion-groups for
    information on how to subscribe to CHICKEN mailing lists.


 2. Installation

    Building CHICKEN requires GNU Make. Other "make" derivates are
    not supported.

    Configuration and customization of the build process is done by
    either using the "configure" script, setting makefile variables
    on the "make" command line or by editing the platform-specific
    makefile.

    2.1. Building from a release tarball

    To build CHICKEN, first extract the archive ("tar xzf
    chicken-<version>.tar.gz" on UNIX or use your favorite
    extraction program on Windows), then change to the
    chicken-<version> directory and invoke "configure" like this:

        ./configure [OPTION ...]

    This will overwrite a configuration file for the subsequent
    "make" invocation that builds the CHICKEN tools and libraries.
    Enter "./configure --help" to see the available options.
    Usually you want to set the installation prefix where the
    results of the build should be installed:

        ./configure --prefix $HOME/.local

    If not given, the installation prefix defaults to "/usr/local".
    You can also define the build platform and whether you want to
    build with debug-information, in addition to other specialized
    settings. The platform is determined automatically if not
    specified.

    Once you have configured the system you can start building it
    by entering

        make

    Note that GNU make is currently required, so use

        gmake

    On systems that use BSD make.

    The old style invocation of "make" is still supported, where
    you pass platform and prefix information on the make command
    line, like this:

        make PLATFORM=<platform> PREFIX=<destination>

    Out-of-directory builds are currently not supported, so you must
    be in the toplevel source directory to invoke "configure" and
    "make".

    If CHICKEN somehow detects the wrong platform, type

        ./configure --platform=list

    to get a list of available platforms, and re-run "configure" using
    the correct platform.

    If you invoke "make" later with different configuration parameters,
    it is advisable to run:

        make confclean

    to remove old configuration files.

    2.2. Building from Git

    If you build CHICKEN directly from the development sources out
    of the git repository, you will need a "chicken" executable to
    generate the compiled C files from the Scheme library sources.
    See section 2.1 for platform detection issues.

    Configuration is the same as when building from the distribution
    tarball. You may have to use the "--chicken" option to define
    which "chicken" compiler binary should be used to translate the
    Scheme source code in the repository:

        ./configure --prefix ... --chicken <install-dir>/bin/chicken

    If you do not set it, the build uses whichever "chicken" is
    found on PATH. Note that this is the compiler that translates
    the Scheme sources, so it stays in use for the whole build: the
    "chicken" that the build itself produces is not used to generate
    the C files. If you are changing the compiler sources -- say
    "c-platform.scm" or "core.scm" -- the effect of your change will
    not appear in the generated C until you build a second time with
    the newly built compiler:

        make CHICKEN=./chicken

    For the same reason, when you want to measure or inspect
    generated code, first let "make" reach a state where it has
    nothing left to do. Under "make -j" the rule that regenerates a
    ".c" file does not depend on the "chicken" binary being relinked
    first, so a parallel build can translate sources with the
    previous compiler.

    If you do not want to use the "configure" script, create an empty
    "config.make", like this:

        touch config.make

    "Config.make" is required by the build process, even if it
    contains no settings.

    If you are building in a checkout where you have built other
    versions of chicken, you need to make sure that all traces of
    the previous build are removed. "make clean" is insufficient,
    and you should do the following:

        make spotless

    You will need to have a "chicken" binary installed, ideally
    from the development snapshot tarball that is closest to the
    git version you are trying to build (significantly older or
    newer ones are unlikely to work), and then use that chicken
    to build from your git sources.  Installing this CHICKEN is
    recommended, if necessary you can install it to a temporary
    location in your homedir for example.

    In some cases, the sources may have diverged enough to
    become unbuildable even with the snapshot.  Then you'll need
    to first build a bootstrapping compiler with the installed
    CHICKEN and then use that to build the version from git.
    See the "Bootstrapping" section below.

    2.3. Verifying your installation is correct

    You can check whether CHICKEN is functioning correctly by
    running

        make check

    This will run the test scripts, which show a lot of output.
    The only thing that matters is the exit status at the end.
    If it exits with status 0, everything is fine, if it exits
    with a nonzero status, the failing test's output should be
    the final lines before Make's "error; exit" output.  If the
    check fails on unmodified sources, please file a bugreport.

    2.4. Finishing the installation

    If CHICKEN is built successfully, you can install it on your
    system by entering

        make install

    To install CHICKEN for a particular prefix on a different
    location, set the "DESTDIR" variable: It designates the
    directory where the files are installed into.

    2.5. Optional features

    You can further enable various optional features by adding
    one or more of the following variables to the "configure"
    invocation:

    --debugbuild
      Disable optimizations in compiled C code and enable
      debug information.

    --staticbuild
      Build only static versions of the runtime library, compiler
      and interpreter.

    --program-prefix PREFIX
      A prefix to prepend to the names of all generated executables.
      This allows having multiple CHICKEN versions in your PATH
      (but note that they have to be installed at different locations).

    --program-suffix SUFFIX
      A suffix to be appended to the names of all generated executables.

    --host SYSTEM
      A "<machine>-<platform>" name prefix to use for the C compiler to
      use to compile the runtime system and executables. Set this variable
      if you want to compile CHICKEN for a different architecture than
      the one on which you are building it.

    --target SYSTEM
      Similar to "HOSTSYSTEM", but specifies the name
      prefix to use for compiling code with the "csc" compiler
      driver. This is required for creating a "cross chicken", a
      specially built CHICKEN that invokes a cross C compiler to
      build the final binaries. You will need a cross compiled
      runtime system by building a version of CHICKEN with the
      "HOST" option mentioned above. More information about this
      process and the variables that you should set are provided
      in the manual (see the "Cross development" chapter).

    --srcdir DIRECTORY
      Specifies that CHICKEN should be built outside of its source
      tree. The SRCDIR variable indicates the location of the
      CHICKEN source tree. The executables and object files will
      be generated in the current directory.

    --vardir DIRECTORY
      If set, this directory overrides the location where
      extensions along with their metadata are stored. Normally
      this will be equivalent to "<PREFIX>/lib/chicken/<BINARYVERSION>".
      When VARDIR is specified, extensions will be stored in
      "<VARDIR>/chicken/<BINARYVERSION>", conforming to the FHS.

    --c-compiler PROGRAM
      If set, determines the C compiler used to compiler C sources.

    --linker PROGRAM
      Determines the executable used for linking compiled object files,
      usually the same as the C compiler executable.

    --profile-object "FILENAME ..."
      This option allows you to profile (parts of) CHICKEN itself.
      Just pass in a whitespace-separated list of objects, without
      the .scm-extension.  (An "object" here is an individual
      .scm-file which gets compiled to a .c-file)
      To build with profiling support, run "make spotless" first.
      Be warned that this is a highly experimental option and
      profiling doesn't work for every component of CHICKEN.

    The "configure" script respects the "CC" and "CFLAGS" environment
    variables, which correspond to the "--c-compiler" option and to
    the "C_COMPILER_OPTIMIZATION_OPTIONS" make variable respectively.
    Note that "CFLAGS" *replaces* the optimization options rather
    than adding to them; see section 2.6.

    Less frequently used settings are available by variables that can
    be passed on the "make" command line:

    2.6. Choosing a C compiler and optimization flags

    The default compiler is "gcc" ("cc" on *BSD systems). CHICKEN
    can be built with the LLVM version of gcc and with "clang",
    the LLVM-based C compiler, just set C_COMPILER to "llvm-gcc"
    or "clang". CHICKEN has also been successfully built with "zig cc",
    the C frontend of the "Zig" language toolchain.

    Both "gcc" and "clang" are supported and both pass "make check".
    Which of the two produces a faster CHICKEN depends on the
    workload and on the compiler versions involved, and the
    difference is normally small, so there is no general
    recommendation: use what your platform provides, and measure
    with your own programs if it matters to you.

    The optimization flags default to "-Os -fomit-frame-pointer",
    or to "-O3 -fomit-frame-pointer" when "OPTIMIZE_FOR_SPEED" is
    set, or to "-O0 -g ..." for a "--debugbuild". The C sources and
    the C emitted by "chicken" are written and tested against these
    defaults.

    Beware that "CFLAGS", and the "C_COMPILER_OPTIMIZATION_OPTIONS"
    variable it sets, *replace* the default optimization flags
    instead of extending them. Passing "CFLAGS=-march=native"
    therefore also turns off "-Os -fomit-frame-pointer". To add a
    flag rather than replace the set, spell out the whole list:

        make C_COMPILER_OPTIMIZATION_OPTIONS="-Os -fomit-frame-pointer -g"

    (The separate "C_COMPILER_OPTIONS" variable, which carries
    "-fno-strict-aliasing -fwrapv", is not affected by "CFLAGS" and
    should be left alone: the generated C relies on both.)

    A few flags are known to cause trouble and are worth avoiding:

    -ffast-math, -Ofast
      These relax floating-point semantics for the whole build. The
      bulk operations in "(chicken number-vector)" document results
      that are bit-for-bit identical with the equivalent
      element-at-a-time Scheme loop, and these flags break that
      guarantee. The kernels defend themselves on gcc, but on clang
      no construct in the source can, because the fusion is a
      back-end decision. If you must build this way, adding
      "-ffp-contract=fast-honor-pragmas" makes clang honour the
      pragma the kernels use and restores at least that much.

    -march=..., -mtune=... for anything above your baseline
      Whatever flags CHICKEN is built with are recorded as
      "C_INSTALL_CFLAGS" in "chicken-config.h" and are then reused
      to compile every egg installed against that CHICKEN. A
      "-march" chosen for the build machine thus propagates to all
      of them and makes the whole installation, not just the
      runtime, unusable on older CPUs of the same family. This is a
      decision for whoever packages or deploys the build, not a
      default worth setting locally.

    OPTIMIZE_FOR_SPEED
      A higher optimization level is not reliably faster here. Some
      of the numeric kernels in "(chicken number-vector)" are
      hand-unrolled for the SLP vectorizer, and at "-O3" clang also
      runs its loop vectorizer across them, re-tiling loops that are
      already tiled; several of those kernels come out measurably
      slower than at the default "-Os". If you set this, measure it.

    2.7. Building for WebAssembly (Emscripten)

    A configured and built host tree can cross-build CHICKEN to
    WebAssembly with Emscripten (tested with emscripten 6.0.10; the
    tests need node 22 or later, 24.15 for wasm64, and "wasm-serve"
    needs python3).
    By default the target is wasm64: 64-bit WebAssembly (memory64),
    on which CHICKEN is a 64-bit platform, with 62-bit fixnums and
    a heap that may grow past 4 GB.  It runs in Chrome and Edge 137,
    Firefox 134 and node 24.15 or later (Chrome and Edge 133 and
    node 24.0 with WASM_SJLJ=wasm-legacy), but not in Safari (up to
    27.2; only Safari Technology Preview has memory64 so far).
    "WASM_ARCH=wasm32" builds the 32-bit target instead, which runs
    in all of them (see WASM_ARCH below), and
    WASM_WEB_ARCHS="wasm64 wasm32" a web page with both, which runs
    wasm64 where the browser can and wasm32 elsewhere (see the web
    page below).
    The build always happens out of tree, by
    default in "build-wasm"; the host tree's own "chicken"
    translates the Scheme sources, so build the host first:

        ./configure && make
        source /path/to/emsdk/emsdk_env.sh    # or pass EMCC=/path/to/emcc
        make wasm                             # everything, in build-wasm/
        make wasm-check                       # node-based tests
        make wasm-serve                       # serve the REPL page (PORT=8080)

    This produces:

    build-wasm/libchicken-static.a
      The runtime library, static only (there is no dynamic loading).

    build-wasm/node/{csi,chicken,chicken-profile,chicken-status}
      The tools, as shell wrappers around node (".js" plus ".wasm"),
      with access to the host file system, environment and exit
      status.  The import libraries are in build-wasm/stage.  Set
      "NODE" to choose the node binary and "NODE_OPTIONS_WASM" to
      pass it options.

    build-wasm/web/
      A static web site: a REPL running the unmodified "csi" in a
      Web Worker, with streaming output, a Stop button (Ctrl-C),
      file upload, a ".csirc", a "Notebook" tab and a "Compile to
      C" tab that runs the wasm "chicken".  It needs no special
      HTTP headers (no COOP/COEP) but must be served over HTTP, not
      opened from a file:// URL.  Any static web server will do.

      Built with WASM_WEB_ARCHS="wasm64 wasm32", the page has the
      modules (csi and the compiler) of both: those of WASM_ARCH
      beside it, the other's in a directory named after it
      (web/wasm32/ by default).  The other architecture is built in
      a build directory of its own inside this one (arch-wasm32),
      with the same settings and eggs, except that the settings whose
      defaults depend on the architecture take its defaults there
      (WASM_SJLJ, so wasm32 uses the legacy exception handling,
      WASM_FRAME_PAD and WASM_MAXIMUM_MEMORY).  The page runs the
      wasm64 build if the browser validates memory64 and exnref (as
      it needs with the default WASM_SJLJ), else the wasm32 build if
      it validates the legacy exception handling instructions, and
      else says what is missing; the REPL, the notebook kernel and the
      compiler all load the build chosen.  So it runs wasm64 in
      Chrome and Edge 137 and Firefox 134 or later, and wasm32 in
      Chrome and Edge 95 to 136, Firefox 100 to 133 and Safari 15.4
      or later (the modules need 15.2, the page's dialogs 15.4;
      Safari was not tested).  The header shows the build that runs,
      and a note in the REPL says why when it is not the first
      choice.  "?arch=wasm32" or "?arch=wasm64" in the page's URL
      asks for a build ("?arch=auto" for the automatic choice), as
      does the "WebAssembly build" entry of the Settings (kept in the
      browser), which the URL overrides: choosing a build there other
      than the one running reloads the page without "?arch=" (with
      the choice in "?arch=" where the browser keeps no settings),
      while saving the other settings keeps it.  The Settings do not
      offer a build the browser is known not to run.  A build the
      page does not have, or that the browser cannot run, is ignored
      with a note saying so.  A page with one build ignores these
      too.  Firefox warns, once for each module
      it compiles, that the legacy exception handling of the wasm32
      build is deprecated.

      The Notebook tab runs a second csi of its own, started on the
      first visit (what is defined there is not visible in the
      REPL, nor the other way round), one cell at a time: a code
      cell runs with Shift+Enter (and the next cell is selected),
      Ctrl+Enter or "Run all", and a cell that fails cancels the
      cells queued after it.  Definitions persist from cell to
      cell.  Output streams in while a cell runs (keeping the first
      and last 512 KB of its text and 500 of its outputs, with a
      note saying how much was left out), a cell can read
      stdin (a field opens under it), and Stop interrupts it; a
      long primitive that does not yield (a huge bignum) is ended
      by restarting the kernel after 3 s, which loses its state,
      also when it follows a "sleep" or a read in the same cell.
      An incomplete cell (an unclosed paren or string, a quote or
      "#;" at its end, comments aside) evaluates nothing.
      Markdown cells hold text.  "(import notebook)" provides rich
      output: "html" and "svg" (strings or SXML), "markdown",
      "text", "image" (a PNG, JPEG, GIF or WebP bytevector), "table"
      (a list of rows, and optional column names) and "show", which
      displays a value at once and, given an id, replaces the
      earlier display with that id ("clear-output" empties the
      cell's output; output over 4 MB is not shown, and in the
      .csirc it goes to the kernel messages).  Markup is shown only
      after it was rebuilt from an allowlist: no scripts, event
      handlers, style sheets or external resources (nor CSS
      functions that load images, such as "image-set"), SVG "use"
      elements only while the elements they copy (nested ones
      multiply) are no more than the markup's characters, or 2000,
      and filters (SVG or CSS, and shadows) only while together
      they cost no more than about 16 blurs of the output (an
      element past that loses its filter).  csi's toplevel commands
      (",d", ",x") work in cells, whose toplevel is the REPL's
      ("read-line" needs "(import (chicken io))", for example).
      SRFI-18 threads only make progress while some cell runs.  The
      notebook is saved in the browser (outputs up to 64 KB per
      cell) and can be exported
      and imported as a ".scm" file (cells start at ";; %%" lines,
      text cells at ";; %% [markdown]"; "csi -s" runs it if it does
      not use the notebook module), or as ".json", with outputs; a
      ".scm" file without such lines is split into cells at blank
      lines.  Import reads files of up to 5 MB and 5000 cells, and
      keeps up to 1000 outputs of a cell (the first ones and the
      last), so an export to ".json" keeps all the outputs only
      while they fit, and else caps them per cell (a toast says
      so); either export says when Import cannot read the file
      back.  In the notebook, Esc and then "?" lists the
      keyboard shortcuts.  The kernel is csi running
      "-e (##webnb#kernel)" (emscripten/webnb.scm): the page posts
      requests to a mailbox in C and reads events as JSON lines on
      file descriptor 3 (emscripten/web/nb-kernel.js).  Of the
      settings' csi arguments it takes the "-:" runtime options,
      -R, -I, -K, -D, -no-feature, -w, -i, -r7rs-syntax and
      -no-parentheses-synonyms; settings apply after "Restart".

    build-wasm/stage/chicken/
      An SDK for compiling your own programs to WebAssembly: the
      library, the headers, the import libraries and "csc-wasm", a
      small csc that translates with the host chicken and compiles
      and links with emcc:

        build-wasm/stage/chicken/bin/csc-wasm -node foo.scm -o foo.js
        node foo.js

      Without "-node" the output uses emscripten's defaults (runs on
      the web, in a worker or under node, with an in-memory file
      system).  Programs are linked statically: a unit a program
      uses must be compiled with "-c -unit NAME" and named with
      "-uses NAME" and its object file.  The comment at the top of
      the "csc-wasm" script lists its options.  By hand, in the
      stage directory, the same is:

        CHICKEN_REPOSITORY_PATH=lib/chicken/12 chicken foo.scm \
          -static $(cat share/chicken/features) -output-file foo.c
        emcc $(cat share/chicken/emcflags) -DHAVE_CHICKEN_CONFIG_H \
          -I include/chicken -c foo.c -o foo.o
        emcc $(cat share/chicken/emldflags) foo.o \
          lib/libchicken-static.a -lm -o foo.js

    "make wasm-cross" additionally builds CHICKEN's own cross
    toolchain (see the "Cross development" chapter of the manual)
    for this SDK, installed in "build-wasm-cross/inst" with the
    program prefix "wasm-".  There is no shared library: the SDK's
    lib/libchicken.a, which "wasm-csc" links without "-static", is
    the static library, so programs are linked statically either
    way.  Add
    "-L @build-wasm-cross/inst/share/wasm-chicken/node.rsp" to
    link for node as "csc-wasm -node" does, and
    "-I build-wasm/stage/chicken/share/chicken" for the Scheme
    include files of eggs (see below).  The toolchain refers
    to the SDK in WASM_BUILDDIR by absolute path, so rerun
    "make wasm-cross" after cleaning that directory.

    Using eggs

    Eggs made only of Scheme code can be loaded from source, as
    anywhere: "(load "matchable.scm")" in the node csi (which sees
    the host file system), or, in the browser REPL, after uploading
    the file.  Eggs with compiled code (foreign code, "##core#inline",
    C files), and eggs that need one, cannot be interpreted; they
    are linked in statically instead:

        make wasm WASM_EGGS="srfi-1 srfi-13 srfi-69 ./my-egg"

    Each entry is an egg name or a local egg directory (holding its
    ".egg" file; with a "/" or relative to the host tree).  Named
    eggs, and all egg dependencies that are not given as
    directories, come from the chicken-install cache (as filled by
    "chicken-install -r"); a missing egg is retrieved into it with
    the host tree's chicken-install.  The eggs are built, in
    dependency order, by the host tree's csi running
    emscripten/wasm-eggs.scm, which follows chicken-install's
    static build of the ".egg" files: every extension becomes a
    unit compiled with csc-wasm (with the egg's csc-options and
    cond-expand clauses evaluated for the target), "c-object"
    components are compiled with emcc and linked with the
    extensions listing them, and test-dependencies are ignored, as
    are dependencies on "chicken" and on modules of the core system
    (such as srfi-4) or mapped away by setup.defaults (such as
    r7rs).  Build-dependencies are needed at build time, on the
    host, as chicken-install installs them before it builds an egg:
    they must be installed in the host repository
    (WASM_HOST_REPOSITORY, see below), where the host chicken
    compiling the eggs finds them; the build stops early, naming the
    egg, when one is not there, and warns when it is older than asked
    for.  They are also built for WebAssembly, like dependencies, as
    importing a compiled module needs its unit at run time even if
    only its syntax is used; one that cannot be (not in the cache
    and not retrieved, or refused as below) is only used on the
    host, with a warning, and an egg that imports one of its
    compiled modules then fails to link.  Unlike chicken-install,
    it does not check the versions
    of dependencies: the release in the cache (or the local
    directory) is built, with a warning if it is older than the
    one asked for; refresh the cache with "chicken-install -r".
    The units are linked into the node csi and the browser REPL and
    initialised when first imported; the import libraries of all
    the modules they define (and their types files) go into the
    repository image, so a plain "(import srfi-1)" works in both.
    The SDK gets the egg objects as lib/libchicken-eggs.a, for
    programs:

        csc-wasm -node prog.scm \
          build-wasm/stage/chicken/lib/libchicken-eggs.a -o prog.js

    The build stops with a message naming the egg for what cannot
    be done statically for WebAssembly: custom build scripts
    (egg-wide or per component, as in "sendfile"), generated source
    files and extensions without static linkage.  Programs are not
    built, and "data" components are not installed (a warning says
    so).  "c-include" and "scheme-include" files are installed where
    the eggs built after them include them from (build-wasm/eggs/
    prefix), and at the same place in the SDK (by default
    include/chicken, on emcc's include path, and share/chicken,
    which csc-wasm puts on the include path after the "-I"
    directories), so that programs can include them.  The Scheme
    files on the include path (in share/chicken) also go into the
    web image, for the REPL, the notebook and the compiler there,
    and the node tools search the SDK's share/chicken unless
    CHICKEN_INCLUDE_PATH is set: "(include "FILE")" finds them, as
    natively.  An egg without
    extensions for the target (a host-only egg, such as "bind")
    builds nothing, with a warning.  An egg that runs code of
    another egg at compile time ("import-for-syntax", as in
    "defstruct") needs that egg compiled for the host: the host
    chicken finds it in the host repository (WASM_HOST_REPOSITORY:
    by default $CHICKEN_REPOSITORY_PATH or the repository of the
    host tree's PREFIX), so install it there with the host
    chicken-install.  csc-wasm looks there too when
    CHICKEN_REPOSITORY_PATH is not set, for programs importing such
    eggs; the compiler of the browser page and the node "chicken"
    cannot load host code, so they cannot expand an import of
    these eggs.

    The node wrappers (node/csi etc.) put the staged repository
    before any CHICKEN_REPOSITORY_PATH of the environment, since
    only it holds the import libraries of the eggs linked in.

    WASM_EGGS is recorded in the build directory apart from the
    other settings: changing it rebuilds only the eggs that change
    (including eggs taken from another directory than before), the
    repository image and the programs linking them.  The host
    settings the eggs are planned with, the chicken-install cache
    (CHICKEN_EGG_CACHE, else $XDG_CACHE_HOME or ~/.cache, followed
    by "/chicken-install", as chicken-install finds it) and
    WASM_HOST_REPOSITORY, are recorded apart again: when one
    changes, the eggs are planned anew, and only those that now come
    from another directory are rebuilt.  The eggs are
    built in build-wasm/eggs.  Tested with srfi-1, srfi-13,
    srfi-14, srfi-18, srfi-19, srfi-69, matchable, miscmacros,
    regex, defstruct, vector-lib, uri-generic, uri-common, base64,
    check-errors, intarweb and memory-mapped-files.

    Other targets: "make wasm-clean" removes the build outputs,
    "make wasm-distclean" the build directories.  "WASM_GOAL"
    selects what "make wasm" builds in the build directory
    ("wasm-lib", "wasm-node", "wasm-web", "wasm-sdk" or the default
    "wasm-all").

    Settings are given on the host "make" command line and recorded
    in the build directory, so that changing one rebuilds
    everything there and repeating it rebuilds nothing.  They are
    recorded, not remembered: every "make wasm..." command for a
    build directory must repeat them, since a setting left out
    reverts to its default (and so rebuilds everything):

    WASM_BUILDDIR
      The build directory (default "build-wasm").

    WASM_PREFIX
      The installation prefix inside the virtual file system
      (default "/chicken").

    WASM_ARCH
      "wasm64" (the default) or "wasm32".  wasm64 needs memory64 in
      the engine (see above); the browser page says so, and suggests
      a wasm32 build, in a browser without it.  wasm32 is a 32-bit
      platform (30-bit fixnums, at most 2 GB of memory) that runs
      wherever WebAssembly does.  The features "wasm64" or "wasm32"
      and "64bit" or "32bit" tell them apart, as (machine-type)
      does.  To keep both, give each its own WASM_BUILDDIR:

        make wasm-check WASM_BUILDDIR=build-wasm32 WASM_ARCH=wasm32

      wasm64 is somewhat slower: in node 25 it took 7% to 17% more
      time than wasm32 on the benchmarks tried (compiled and
      interpreted fib, fft, sorting, the wasm "chicken" compiling
      tests/compiler.scm), except bignum arithmetic, which was
      about 20% faster with 64-bit digits.

    WASM_WEB_ARCHS
      The architectures of the web page (default: WASM_ARCH alone),
      which must include WASM_ARCH; when only this is given,
      WASM_ARCH is its first one.  "wasm64 wasm32" builds the other
      one too, for the page only (see build-wasm/web above).  It is
      recorded apart from the other settings: changing it builds or
      removes the other architecture's modules, and rebuilds only the
      page's index.html here.  The second build compiles the runtime library and the
      page's modules once more, and the page takes about twice the
      space (21 MB instead of 11).

    WASM_STACK_SIZE
      The link-time stack size, which also bounds the nursery
      (default 4194304 bytes).  A "-:s" nursery larger than fits
      is refused at startup.

    WASM_INITIAL_MEMORY, WASM_MAXIMUM_MEMORY
      Linear memory (defaults 32 MB, and 16 GB on wasm64, the most
      Chrome and node allow, or 2 GB on wasm32; the heap grows
      between them).  The heap is two semispaces, and growing it
      needs the old and the new heap at once, so the live data
      that fits is well below the maximum.  The heap grows only as
      far as both its new halves surely fit in the memory left
      (including what malloc holds free, as after the heap
      shrank), and then stops with "out of memory - heap has reached its
      maximum size (WebAssembly memory is limited to N bytes)".
      With 16 GB, a single object of up to nearly 8 GB works;
      live data made of many smaller objects stops at about 4 GB,
      as the earlier heaps leave memory fragmented.

    WASM_SJLJ
      How setjmp and longjmp (which the runtime uses for every
      minor GC) are compiled:
        "wasm" (the default on wasm64): WebAssembly exception
          handling with exnref, the standard instructions; needs
          Chrome and Edge 137, Firefox 131, Safari 18.4, node 22.19
          (in the 22 series) or 24.15 or later.
        "wasm-legacy" (the default on wasm32): the legacy
          exception handling instructions, which run in Chrome 95,
          Firefox 100, Safari 15.2, node 17 or later, but which
          Firefox reports as deprecated, with a console warning for
          each module it compiles.
        "emscripten": emscripten's emulation through JavaScript
          exceptions, for engines without wasm exception handling.
      With "wasm", the browser page says so, and suggests
      "wasm-legacy", in a browser without exnref.  "wasm" and
      "wasm-legacy" ran equally fast (median times within 3%, about
      the run-to-run noise) on compiled fib and fft and on
      interpreted fib, in node 25 on wasm64.

    WASM_FRAME_PAD
      The minimum shadow-stack bytes per CPS frame (default 128 on
      wasm64, 64 on wasm32), which keeps the engine's native call
      depth bounded by the nursery.  For the same reason the
      nursery is limited to 3072 times the pad on wasm64 (384 KB
      by default) and 4096 times on wasm32 (256 KB), whatever the
      stack size: a larger "-:s" is refused at startup, and a
      larger "-nursery" is lowered to it.  These limits keep a
      margin of 1.6 to 1.9 over the nursery that overflowed a 1 MB
      (browser worker) stack in the tests.

    WASM_SIMD
      Compile with -msimd128.

    WASM_LINK_OPT
      The emcc link optimization options (default -O2).

    WASM_DEBUG
      A debug build (-O0 -g, emscripten ASSERTIONS=2).

    WASM_EGGS
      Eggs to link into csi (see "Using eggs" above; recorded apart
      from the other settings).

    EMCC, WASM_HOST_CHICKEN
      The emcc to use (default: from $EMSDK, else "emcc" on the
      PATH) and the host chicken that translates the sources
      (default: the in-tree "chicken").

    "make wasm-check" runs tests/wasm-smoke.sh (which first checks
    that the tools, the page's modules and a program compiled with
    csc-wasm are all of WASM_ARCH, and the modules of the page's
    other architecture of that one), the interpreted and
    compiled test lanes of tests/runtests-wasm.sh, the headless
    REPL tests tests/wasm/repl-harness.js (also under Liftoff with a
    small stack) and tests/wasm/worker-harness.js, the notebook
    kernel tests tests/wasm/notebook-harness.js (also under Liftoff),
    these three again with "--arch=A" on the modules of the other
    architecture A of a page with two builds (wasm32 with the default
    WASM_ARCH), and an eggs lane:
    it builds csi and the REPL again, in tests-run/eggs-build of the
    build directory, with the test eggs of tests/wasm/eggs, and runs
    tests/wasm-eggs.sh (node csi, programs compiled with csc-wasm,
    the eggs' include files, a build-dependency installed in a host
    repository and built from an egg cache, replanning, refused eggs and
    tests/wasm/egg-harness.js and
    notebook-harness.js for the REPL and the notebook).  The browser test
    tests/wasm/page.spec.mjs needs Playwright and is run by hand:

        BROWSERS=chromium,firefox node tests/wasm/page.spec.mjs build-wasm/web

    For a page with two builds (make wasm WASM_WEB_ARCHS="wasm64
    wasm32"), it tests the wasm64 build, then the wasm32 one as a
    browser without memory64 or exnref gets it, the lockout without
    any exception handling, ?arch= and the Settings choice; the
    legacy exception handling warnings of the wasm32 pages are only
    counted.  Run it on such a page and on one with a single build.

    Limitations:

      - There is no dynamic loading: no ".so" files, and eggs with
        compiled code must be linked statically (WASM_EGGS).  "csc"
        and "chicken-install" do not run inside WebAssembly; use
        "csc-wasm" or "make wasm-cross".

      - "fork", "exec", "process", pipes and signals raise errors,
        there are no sockets in browsers, and profiling ("-:p")
        does not work.  In the browser "system", ",e" and ",s" fail.

      - "executable-pathname" is #f, and "cpu-time" and
        "current-process-milliseconds" measure wall-clock time.
        On wasm32 "current-jiffy" counts milliseconds, not
        microseconds.

      - Local time is the JavaScript engine's: in node that of TZ
        or of the system, in the browser the browser's.  The C
        library ignores TZ, and "local-timezone-abbreviation" gives
        names like "UTC+0200".  When TZ is not set, the runtime sets
        it to the engine's time zone (such as "Europe/Rome"), which
        is what the locale egg (used by srfi-19) needs.

      - Runtime options ("-:...") must precede all other arguments.

      - A fault in unsafe code (e.g. "fx/" by zero compiled with
        -unsafe) aborts the instance instead of raising an error,
        and wild writes into linear memory go unnoticed.  On wasm32
        blocks are limited to 16 MB, as on any 32-bit platform.

      - In the browser REPL, long primitives (a huge "expt", "sort"
        or "make-list") do not yield: Stop then replaces the worker
        after 3 seconds (whatever the REPL showed last, such as
        "sleeping").  SRFI-18 threads are suspended while the
        REPL waits for input, and "emergency-exit" may lose buffered
        output.  Closing a standard port (as "call-with-port" does)
        closes it until the next prompt, or in the notebook until
        the cell ends (where a native csi would report its closed
        input port again and again).

    2.8. Uninstalling CHICKEN

    To remove CHICKEN from your file-system, enter (probably as
    root):

        make uninstall

    (If you gave DESTDIR during installation, you have to pass
    the same setting to "make" when uninstalling)

